<%# The ladder is an approximation of an opaque mechanic, so it says so
    rather than presenting itself as a hard rule.
    Locals: kind — :penetration (a round, estimated), :effectiveness (a round,
    from the wiki chart) or :protection (a plate) %>
<% kind ||= :penetration %>
<details class="disclosure mt-2">
  <summary class="text-[0.6875rem] text-[var(--text-muted)]">How this is worked out</summary>
  <p class="mt-1.5 max-w-prose text-xs leading-relaxed text-[var(--text-muted)]">
    <% if kind == :protection %>
      Armor is rated 1 to 6, and a plate stops most rounds below its own class.
      Each step up needs roughly 10 more penetration to defeat, so a class 6
      plate takes about 60 penetration to be beaten reliably. Plates also lose
      durability as they absorb hits, and the rating above is the plate's, not
      the whole vest's.
    <% elsif kind == :effectiveness %>
      The <%= link_to "wiki's ballistics chart", "https://escapefromtarkov.fandom.com/wiki/Ballistics",
            class: "underline hover:text-[var(--accent)]", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>
      rates every round 0 to 6 against each armor class, from "Pointless" (0)
      to "Usually ignores" (6). Level 4, "Effective", is where the round
      penetrates: it stops 3 to 5 rounds on average, and quickly does better as
      the plate wears. The levels above are that rating, class by class; a
      round still lands reduced damage on a plate it does not penetrate.
    <% else %>
      Penetration decides which armor plates a round defeats. Each class step
      needs roughly 10 more penetration than the one below it, so 31 beats
      class 3 reliably and only has a chance against class 4. Rounds that do
      not penetrate still land reduced damage, and a worn plate is easier to
      get through. This round has no row on the wiki's ballistics chart, so
      this is the estimate.
    <% end %>
    These bands are the community's working approximation of an opaque
    in-game calculation, not official numbers.
  </p>
</details>
